Your specific situation — the backup BIOS and integrity check:

Your Dell 5480 has a dual-BIOS architecture with recovery partition. The relevant concern given your threat model is whether the primary BIOS image itself has been modified. Dell's integrity check compares against the backup — but if both copies were modified simultaneously (which ME has the capability to do), the integrity check passes against a compromised baseline.
bash

# Read current BIOS flash regions
dnf install flashrom

# CRITICAL: Do NOT write with flashrom without knowing exactly what you're doing
# READ ONLY first
flashrom -p internal --ifd -r /tmp/bios_full_dump.bin 2>&1
# This may fail with "Root Complex" error on locked ME regions — expected

# If it reads, check the ME region size
flashrom -p internal --ifd 2>&1 | grep -E 'Region|ME|BIOS|GbE'

# The Intel Flash Descriptor will show which regions are read/write locked
# ME region is typically locked from host CPU access by descriptor policy

Intel ME tools:
bash

# MEInfo — Intel's own ME diagnostic
# Not in RHEL repos, must download from Intel or extract from Dell BIOS package
# But inxi and dmidecode can show ME version
dmidecode -t 0 | grep -i 'firmware\|ME\|version'

# MEI version via kernel
cat /sys/class/mei/mei0/fw_ver 2>/dev/null
# Shows ME firmware version directly
# Compare against known-good version for your platform on Intel's ARK database

# Also:
dmesg | grep -i 'mei\|ME firmware\|AMT'

The downgrade path specifically:

Given your threat model the downgrade option in BIOS is worth using if you have a known-good earlier version. The procedure:
bash

# 1. Get your service tag
dmidecode -s system-serial-number

# 2. Check ME version
cat /sys/class/mei/mei0/fw_ver

# 3. Download target BIOS version from Dell's driver matrix for your service tag
# Use the version *before* any anomalous behavior began

# 4. Disable Secure Flash if it blocks downgrade
# This is a BIOS option: Security -> BIOS Downgrade -> Allow
# Your system already has this per your description

# 5. Flash via fwupd with force flag
fwupdmgr install <path-to-.cab-or-.exe> --force --allow-older

Verify BIOS integrity from running system:
bash

# Compare SMBIOS data against Dell's known values for your model
dmidecode > /tmp/smbios_current.txt
# Key fields to verify:
# BIOS Version — should match Dell's published version string exactly
# BIOS Release Date
# System UUID — should be stable
# Check for any vendor string anomalies

# Intel BIOS Guard status
dmidecode -t 0 | grep -i 'BIOS Guard\|Secure\|Guard'

# Check EFI variables for BIOS modification indicators
efivar -l 2>/dev/null | grep -iE 'dell\|setup\|secureboot'
efibootmgr -v

The filesystem backup BIOS option — Dell stores a BIOS recovery image in a hidden FAT partition on the drive, separate from the OS. On some models it's also in the service partition. If your threat model includes ME-level compromise the concern is whether this recovery image was also overwritten.
bash

# Find Dell recovery partition
lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,FSTYPE,LABEL,PARTLABEL
fdisk -l | grep -i 'EFI\|recovery\|dell'

# Mount and inspect if found
