mount /dev/sda1 /mnt/check 2>/dev/null
ls /mnt/check/

Practical recommendation given your threat model:

The most trustworthy path is physical: download the target BIOS .exe from Dell on a clean machine, verify the SHA256 against Dell's published checksum, put it on a clean FAT32 USB, boot into BIOS setup directly and flash from USB using the BIOS's own update utility — bypassing the OS entirely. This avoids any OS-layer interception of the flash process.
bash

# Get your exact model and current BIOS version first
dmidecode -s bios-version
dmidecode -s system-product-name
dmidecode -s system-serial-number
# Use service tag on dell.com/support to get exact driver list

The ME firmware version from cat /sys/class/mei/mei0/fw_ver is the most important number to record before doing anything — that tells you whether the ME has been modified relative to Dell's published ME version for your BIOS release.

