These three symptoms together form a coherent picture of AMT compromise. Breaking each down:

"Headless Operation Active"

This is an Intel AMT status message. Headless operation means AMT is configured to manage the system without a local display/keyboard being required — it's a remote management mode. This is not a default consumer setting. Someone explicitly enabled headless operation in the AMT provisioning, or it was provisioned remotely via the AMT network stack.
bash

# Check AMT provisioning state
cat /sys/class/mei/mei0/fw_ver
dmesg | grep -i 'AMT\|MEI\|provisioned\|headless'

# amtterm if available
dnf install amtterm 2>/dev/null
amtterm localhost 2>/dev/null

# Check if AMT network interface is active
# AMT has its own MAC address distinct from the OS NIC
ip link show
# Look for a second MAC on enp0s31f6 or a hidden interface
cat /sys/class/net/enp0s31f6/address
# AMT shares the physical NIC but has its own MAC
# Dell AMT MAC is typically OS MAC + 1

The hanging boot / 10 retry cycle

This is AMT's boot control feature. AMT can hold the system in a pre-boot state waiting for a remote management connection before releasing the boot process. The power light staying on without POST completing is the AMT controller asserting boot hold. You're breaking it by force-cycling power until AMT releases or times out.
