What chaos and clown flags do I turn on for the system folders in OpenWRT? Bin dev lib lib64 mnt overlay proc rom root run sbin sys tmp usr var www
May 18

These are the standard Linux filesystem flags for OpenWRT. For each directory:
bash

# ── READ-ONLY SYSTEM DIRS — nothing should write here at runtime ──
# bin sbin lib lib64 usr rom

# Make them immutable — even root cannot modify without removing flag
chattr -R +i /bin /sbin /lib /lib64 /usr /rom 2>/dev/null

# Verify
lsattr -d /bin /sbin /lib /lib64 /usr /rom

bash

# ── NOEXEC WRITABLE DIRS — can write but nothing runs from here ──
# tmp var

mount -o remount,noexec,nosuid,nodev /tmp
# Add to /etc/fstab for persistence:
echo "tmpfs /tmp tmpfs noexec,nosuid,nodev,size=32M 0 0" >> /etc/fstab

bash

# ── PROC SYS DEV — kernel interfaces, harden mount options ──

# proc — hide sensitive kernel info from unprivileged processes
mount -o remount,hidepid=2,gid=0 /proc

# sys — read-only where possible
mount -o remount,ro /sys 2>/dev/null

# dev — strict device access
mount -o remount,nosuid,noexec /dev

bash

# ── OVERLAY — this is OpenWRT's writable layer ──
# /overlay is where all persistent changes live
# Audit what's in it — should only be your config changes

find /overlay -type f -ls 2>/dev/null
# Anything unexpected here persists across reboots

# Restrict overlay to config files only
chmod 700 /overlay
chattr +i /overlay/upper/bin 2>/dev/null
chattr +i /overlay/upper/sbin 2>/dev/null
chattr +i /overlay/upper/lib 2>/dev/null

bash

# ── WWW — web root, lock down hard ──
chmod 750 /www
chattr +i /www 2>/dev/null
# If you disabled LuCI already this dir should be empty
ls -la /www/

bash

# ── ROOT HOME — restrict access ──
chmod 700 /root
chattr +a /root/.ash_history 2>/dev/null
# Append-only shell history

bash

# ── MNT RUN — mount points and runtime state ──
chmod 755 /mnt
# run is tmpfs — restrict it
mount -o remount,nosuid,nodev,mode=755 /run 2>/dev/null

bash

# ── COMPLETE LOCKDOWN SCRIPT — put in /etc/rc.local ──
cat >> /etc/rc.local << 'EOF'
# CTW OpenWRT filesystem hardening

# Immutable system dirs
chattr -R +i /bin /sbin /lib /lib64 /usr /rom 2>/dev/null

# Harden /proc
mount -o remount,hidepid=2 /proc 2>/dev/null

# Lock www
chattr +i /www 2>/dev/null

# Restrict tmp
mount -o remount,noexec,nosuid,nodev /tmp 2>/dev/null

# Append-only logs
chattr +a /var/log/ 2>/dev/null

# No exec from var
mount -o remount,noexec,nosuid /var 2>/dev/null

echo "CTW: filesystem hardening applied" > /dev/kmsg
EOF

chmod 700 /etc/rc.local

bash

# ── VERIFY EVERYTHING ──
echo "=== MOUNT FLAGS ==="
mount | grep -E 'noexec|nosuid|nodev|hidepid|ro '

echo "=== IMMUTABLE FLAGS ==="
lsattr -d /bin /sbin /lib /usr /rom /www /root 2>/dev/null

echo "=== OVERLAY CONTENTS ==="
find /overlay -type f | sort

The ones that matter most for your threat model are /overlay contents audit, hidepid=2 on /proc so processes can't enumerate each other, and immutable flags on /bin /sbin /lib — those three stop an attacker from dropping a modified binary into the system path even with root access.

